It goes way farther in abstraction than that.

The Serializer is a data mapper where one of the mapping direction is a completely different format, but the in-between format (the "normalized" one) is a PHP array.

However, we might identify other cases:

* An API Platform resource can also be a data-mapped object, thanks to the ApiResource <-> Doctrine Entity relation
* An HTML form can also be a data-mapped object: as there is the `data_class` object, you come from a normalized (array) format to a PHP one
* A CRUD-admin-generator is also a data mapper, most of them would use forms anyway
* A queue system can also be a data mapper, since you have the storage's data format (rabbitmq, redis, etc.) and your PHP object. One could use serialization, or could just map the native data to an SDK-like library object.
* You could need a data mapper to map your own domain-objects into an API/SDK/other resource object
* And other cases I'm sure I'm not aware of

We use data mappers every day in our apps and sometimes without realizing how abstract we could bring this.

